speechtext:
Under the Workmen's Compensation Act in British Columbia the workmen's compensation is recognized as the first charge against the assets of any company in default for any reason. If the company cannot meet its liabilities the workmen's compensation is the first charge, even before deferred wages. Under that act the employee has no responsibility whatsoever and the employer himself collects from the employee certain sums which in turn are handed over to the workmen's compensation board, the board accepting full responsibility for the man's injury. In connection with this particular clause, I am sure that the employer himself would much prefer to have the responsibility of -collecting the money and turning it over to the board rather than having to be bothered with his employees coming in and asking -for stamps, and having to provide stamps for them, and things of that sort. The government could very well put -this insurance on such a basis that it becomes one of the first charges against any company, thus giving the employee the insurance to which he is entitled and which he would not otherwise
92582-97}
have. It could not be real insurance if there were any question about it, and on the interpretation the government is placing on the wording in this case, there would be question about it.
